      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme risks trivializing or sensationalizing the profound suffering of Holocaust survivors. Its potential for misuse can contribute to Holocaust denial or minimization, which can cause emotional harm to survivors and their families and distort historical understanding.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_9d9a5540"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a03f208b",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "audience description",
      "description": "The target community is individuals who are aware of or interested in historical atrocities, particularly the Holocaust, and may be engaging with content that references or depicts its victims. This includes educators, students, historians, activists, and the general public with an interest in genocide, human rights, or WWII history. The meme's use of the image of child survivors behind barbed wire, combined with the text 'child survivors of holocaust,' is designed to evoke emotional responses and provoke discussion about the Holocaust, its victims, and its legacy. The intended audience likely includes those who understand the gravity of the Holocaust and are capable of interpreting the image as a historical document rather than a sensationalized or trivialized visual. The meme may also be targeted at those who are familiar with internet meme culture and are seeking content that is provocative or emotionally charged, even if it risks being harmful or insensitive. The community-specific element is the historical and cultural reference to the Holocaust, which requires prior knowledge to fully comprehend the context and gravity of the image. The demographic cues suggest an audience that is likely to be older, educated, and engaged with historical or political discourse, as the subject matter is not suitable for younger audiences without context. The cultural reference to the Holocaust is a powerful and sensitive topic that is often used in educational and commemorative contexts, indicating that the target audience may be those who are familiar with such contexts and are capable of engaging with the content in a thoughtful and respectful manner.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_9d9a5540"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    }
